<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pham Van Dong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1906–2000</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Phạm Văn Đồng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pham Van Dong was a Vietnamese communist leader and close associate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> delegation at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Conference of 1954 and was prime minister of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from 1955 and of reunified Vietnam until 1987, one of the longest-serving heads of government of the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pham-van-dong.jpg" alt="Pham Van Dong" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ph%E1%BA%A1m_V%C4%83n_%C4%90%E1%BB%93ng_1972.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
